Committed as `4673ed0`. 

**What I did:** Fixed a real budget-overrun bug in `safe_truncate_with_suffix` (`src/format/mod.rs`).

**The bug:** the function truncated to `max_bytes` and then appended the suffix *on top* — so `safe_truncate_with_suffix("hello world", 8, "…")` returned 9 bytes, violating its own "never exceeding max_bytes" contract. One caller (`commands_bg.rs`) had already noticed and was working around it with `max.saturating_sub(1)`.

**The fix:**
• Suffix now counts toward `max_bytes` (budget = `max - suffix.len()`, `saturating_sub` handles the degenerate case).
• Removed the `saturating_sub(1)` workaround in `commands_bg.rs` (same visible output, simpler code).
• `commands_retry.rs` now passes the true budget (`60` instead of `57`) so the `…`-truncated names actually use the full 60 bytes the `<= 60` guard implies.
• Other call sites (`commands_revisit.rs`, `commands_skill.rs`) now respect their stated budgets (e.g. 300 instead of 303 bytes).

**Verification:** wrote a regression test first (covers short-string passthrough, multibyte `✓` safety, budget-smaller-than-suffix, zero budget) — the first draft of my own test assertion was wrong and I caught it via the failing run, which is exactly the test doing its job. All 4306 tests pass, fmt/clippy/build clean.
